%% -- Introduction -------------------------------------------------------------

\section{Introduction} \label{sec:intro}

In modern evidence-based governance, policy interventions are increasingly decentralized. Monitoring the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), allocating municipal fiscal transfers, targeting social safety net subsidies, and responding to localized disease outbreaks all require dependable statistical indicators at highly disaggregated geographic administrative levels \citep{rao2015small, pfeffermann2013new}. However, national statistical offices (NSOs) face structural sample size limitations. Large-scale household surveys---such as the American Community Survey (ACS) in the United States, the European Union Statistics on Income and Living Conditions (EU-SILC), and the National Socioeconomic Survey (Susenas) in Indonesia---are powered to yield acceptable sampling errors only at national or broad regional levels. When partitioned into sub-domains (e.g., municipalities, districts, or health zones), domain-specific sample sizes become sparse or zero, causing direct survey estimators to suffer from high sampling variance and unacceptable coefficients of variation \citep{burgard2020computational, pratesi2016analysis}.

Small area estimation (SAE) overcomes these limitations by ``borrowing strength'' across domains and time periods via linking models that combine survey responses with auxiliary administrative registers, census aggregates, or geospatial layers \citep{rao2015small}. The cornerstone of this literature remains the area-level Fay-Herriot (FH) model \citep{fay1979estimates}, extended to account for spatial dependencies via Simultaneous Autoregressive (SAR) structures \citep{petrucci2006small, pratesi2008small} and temporal dynamics via panel autoregressive processes \citep{marhuenda2013small}. In the presence of micro-level auxiliary data, the nested error regression model of \citet{battese1988error} (BHF) provides unit-level empirical best linear unbiased predictors (EBLUP).

Despite these theoretical advances, operationalizing small area models within production environments in NSOs has been hindered by three structural bottlenecks:
%
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Frequentist Computational and Memory Bottlenecks}: Existing implementations of spatial and spatio-temporal Fay-Herriot models rely on interpreted languages. Estimating model variance components via Restricted Maximum Likelihood (REML) requires repeated inversions of $D \times D$ or $DT \times DT$ covariance matrices. When evaluating Mean Squared Error (MSE) through parametric or non-parametric bootstrap resampling \citep{gonzalez2008bootstrap}, runtimes escalate to hours. Crucially, memory allocation for dense covariance arrays grows quadratically ($O(D^2)$ to $O(D^2 T^2)$), triggering out-of-memory (OOM) crashes on large national grids ($D \ge 500$).
  \item \textbf{Distributional Inflexibility and MCMC Friction in Bayesian SAE}: Real-world welfare indicators are rarely Gaussian. Rates and proportions (poverty headcounts, unemployment, stunting) are strictly bounded in $(0, 1)$, while expenditures exhibit severe positive skewness (Gamma), and demographic events represent discrete counts (Poisson, Negative Binomial) \citep{janicki2020properties, fabrizi2011hierarchical, boubeta2016empirical}. Under non-Gaussian likelihoods, linear EBLUP is invalid, requiring Empirical Best Prediction (EBP) within a Hierarchical Bayesian (HB) framework. Currently, Bayesian SAE software relies on Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) sampling via \pkg{Stan} or \pkg{JAGS} \citep{denicolo2022tipsae}. MCMC sampling requires lengthy runtimes, complex convergence diagnostics ($\hat{R}$, trace plots), and often suffers from divergent transitions on complex spatial graphs \citep{simpson2017penalising}.
  \item \textbf{The Institutional Benchmarking and Dissemination Gap}: In official statistics, model-based small area estimates must numerically harmonize with published higher-level totals (e.g., provincial and national direct estimates) to preserve institutional credibility and prevent contradictory policy decisions \citep{you2002small, wang2008small, stefan2020benchmarking}. Existing packages rarely offer two-stage hierarchical benchmarking. Furthermore, software ecosystems typically end at model fitting, omitting essential dissemination steps such as automated reliability tagging based on official Relative Standard Error (RSE) thresholds, model concordance evaluation, and publication-ready spatial choropleth mapping.
\end{enumerate}

