As summarized in Table~\ref{tab:pkg_comparison}, the current software landscape is compartmentalized. Foundational toolkits such as \pkg{sae} \citep{molina2015sae} are implemented in interpreted \proglang{R}, lack multi-threading, and are restricted to Gaussian linear frameworks. Packages such as \pkg{emdi} \citep{kreutzmann2019emdi} offer visualizations but lack spatio-temporal dynamics and non-Gaussian spatial Bayesian likelihoods. Specialized packages such as \pkg{tipsae} \citep{denicolo2022tipsae} implement Beta regression but depend on MCMC runtimes that scale poorly for panel graphs. Toolkits such as \pkg{mcmcsae} \citep{boonstra2022mcmcsae} remain simulation-based, while \pkg{SUMMER} \citep{li2020summer} is tailored to demographic survey mortality rather than general survey design-variance matching.

To resolve these limitations, this paper introduces \pkg{fastsae}. Specifically, this paper makes four concrete, testable contributions:
%
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Compiled C++17 Core with OpenMP Acceleration}: We engineer an optimized linear algebra core in \proglang{C++17} via \pkg{RcppArmadillo} that speeds up classical, spatial, and spatio-temporal Fay-Herriot estimation by up to $364\times$ relative to \pkg{sae} and $12,300\times$ relative to \pkg{emdi} at $D=1,000$, while slashing peak memory consumption from gigabytes to under 25 megabytes.
  \item \textbf{Unified Deterministic Bayesian Hierarchical Modeling via INLA with Exact Survey Matching}: We formulate a Hierarchical Bayes interface (\code{hb\_area()}) spanning six probability distributions (Gaussian, Beta, Gamma, Binomial, Poisson, Negative Binomial) powered by Integrated Nested Laplace Approximations \citep{rue2009approximate}. It embeds exact survey variance mappings---notably the \citet{janicki2020properties} Beta dispersion parameterization and Gamma CV matching---achieving $73\times$ to $544\times$ speedups over Stan-based MCMC while maintaining near-perfect inferential concordance ($r = 0.9993$).
  \item \textbf{Two-Stage Hierarchical Benchmarking Engine}: We implement a modular calibration engine (\code{benchmark\_sae()}) that simultaneously enforces consistency across multi-level administrative hierarchies (Level 0 National, Level 1 Provincial, Level 2 District) via ratio, difference, variance-weighted optimal, and logit-scale adjustments.
  \item \textbf{End-to-End Official Statistics Dissemination Suite}: We provide turnkey post-estimation workflows, including automated reliability tagging (\code{add\_reliability\_flags()}) based on official NSO thresholds, model concordance diagnostics (\code{compare\_sae()}), publication-ready choropleth mapping (\code{map\_sae()}), and multi-sheet spreadsheet exports (\code{export\_sae()}).
\end{enumerate}

%% -- Methodological Framework -------------------------------------------------

\section{Methodological framework} \label{sec:methods}

\subsection{Frequentist small area models} \label{sec:methods_freq}

\subsubsection{Classical Fay-Herriot model}
Let $m$ denote the number of small areas indexed by $i = 1, \dots, m$. The classical area-level model \citep{fay1979estimates} is defined by:
%
\begin{equation} \label{eq:fh_model}
y_i = \bm{x}_i^\top \bm{\beta} + u_i + e_i, \quad u_i \overset{\mathrm{iid}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_u^2), \quad e_i \overset{\mathrm{ind}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(0, D_i),
\end{equation}
%
where $y_i$ is the design-based direct estimator of the target indicator $\theta_i$, $\bm{x}_i$ is a $p \times 1$ vector of auxiliary covariates, $D_i$ is the known design variance, and $u_i$ is the area random effect. The Empirical Best Linear Unbiased Predictor (EBLUP) is given by:
%
\begin{equation} \label{eq:fh_eblup}
\hat{\theta}_i = \hat{\gamma}_i y_i + (1 - \hat{\gamma}_i) \bm{x}_i^\top \hat{\bm{\beta}}, \quad \hat{\gamma}_i = \frac{\hat{\sigma}_u^2}{\hat{\sigma}_u^2 + D_i},
\end{equation}
%
where $\hat{\bm{\beta}} = (\bm{X}^\top \hat{\bm{V}}^{-1} \bm{X})^{-1} \bm{X}^\top \hat{\bm{V}}^{-1} \bm{y}$ with $\bm{V} = \mathrm{diag}(\sigma_u^2 + D_1, \dots, \sigma_u^2 + D_m)$. Mean squared error is evaluated via the second-order Prasad-Rao approximation \citep{prasad1990estimation}:
%
\begin{equation} \label{eq:fh_mse}
\mathrm{MSE}(\hat{\theta}_i) \approx g_{1i}(\hat{\sigma}_u^2) + g_{2i}(\hat{\sigma}_u^2) + 2 g_{3i}(\hat{\sigma}_u^2).
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Spatial Fay-Herriot model}
To accommodate spatial dependence across contiguous domains, \citet{petrucci2006small} and \citet{pratesi2008small} modeled the area random effects $\bm{u} = (u_1, \dots, u_m)^\top$ via a Simultaneous Autoregressive (SAR) process:
%
\begin{equation} \label{eq:sfh_sar}
\bm{u} = \rho \bm{W} \bm{u} + \bm{\epsilon}, \quad \bm{\epsilon} \sim \mathcal{N}_m(\bm{0}, \sigma_1^2 \bm{I}_m),
\end{equation}
%
where $\rho$ is the spatial autoregressive parameter and $\bm{W}$ is a row-standardized spatial proximity matrix. The marginal covariance matrix is $\bm{V}(\sigma_1^2, \rho) = \bm{G}(\sigma_1^2, \rho) + \bm{D}$, where $\bm{G}(\sigma_1^2, \rho) = \sigma_1^2 [(\bm{I}_m - \rho \bm{W})^\top (\bm{I}_m - \rho \bm{W})]^{-1}$. The spatial EBLUP is:
%
\begin{equation} \label{eq:sfh_eblup}
\hat{\bm{\theta}} = \bm{X} \hat{\bm{\beta}} + \hat{\bm{G}} \hat{\bm{V}}^{-1} (\bm{y} - \bm{X} \hat{\bm{\beta}}).
\end{equation}
%
Variance components are estimated via profile REML over the valid spectral bounds $\rho \in (\lambda_{\min}^{-1}, \lambda_{\max}^{-1})$.

\subsubsection{Spatio-temporal Fay-Herriot model}
When small area data are collected across longitudinal waves $t = 1, \dots, T$, borrowing strength across space and time yields substantial efficiency gains \citep{marhuenda2013small}:
%
\begin{equation} \label{eq:stfh_model}
y_{it} = \bm{x}_{it}^\top \bm{\beta} + u_{1,it} + u_{2,it} + e_{it},
\end{equation}
%
where $\bm{u}_{1,t} = \rho_1 \bm{W} \bm{u}_{1,t} + \bm{\epsilon}_{1,t}$ represents a spatial SAR process and $u_{2,it} = \rho_2 u_{2,i,t-1} + \epsilon_{2,it}$ follows an AR(1) temporal process with $|\rho_2| < 1$. In \pkg{fastsae}, the block-structured covariance matrix is assembled and inverted using optimized Kronecker routines.

\subsubsection{Unit-level Battese-Harter-Fuller model}
When unit microdata are accessible alongside population auxiliary totals, \pkg{fastsae} implements the nested error regression model \citep{battese1988error}:
%
\begin{equation} \label{eq:bhf_model}
y_{dj} = \bm{x}_{dj}^\top \bm{\beta} + u_d + e_{dj}, \quad d = 1, \dots, D, \quad j = 1, \dots, n_d,
\end{equation}
%
yielding EBLUP predictors $\hat{\bar{Y}}_d = f_d \bar{y}_d + (1 - f_d)(\bar{\bm{X}}_{d, r}^\top \hat{\bm{\beta}} + \hat{\gamma}_d (\bar{y}_d - \bar{\bm{x}}_d^\top \hat{\bm{\beta}}))$, where $f_d = n_d / N_d$.

\subsection{Bayesian non-Gaussian hierarchical EBP via INLA} \label{sec:methods_inla}

\subsubsection{Latent Gaussian models}
For non-Gaussian indicators, \pkg{fastsae} formulates small area estimation within the Latent Gaussian Model (LGM) framework \citep{rue2009approximate}:
%
\begin{equation} \label{eq:lgm_formulation}
y_i \mid \eta_i, \bm{\theta} \sim \pi(y_i \mid \eta_i, \bm{\theta}), \quad \eta_i = g(\mu_i) = \bm{x}_i^\top \bm{\beta} + u_i + v_i,
\end{equation}
%
where $g(\cdot)$ is a monotone link function, $v_i \overset{\mathrm{iid}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(0, \tau_v^{-1})$ is an unstructured effect, and $\bm{u} \sim \mathcal{N}(\bm{0}, \bm{Q}^{-1}(\bm{\theta}_s))$ is a spatial Gaussian Markov Random Field (GMRF). By default, \pkg{fastsae} utilizes the Full Laplace approximation (\code{strategy = "laplace"}) rather than the simplified Laplace expansion, guaranteeing high analytical fidelity even near boundary domains.

\subsubsection{Exact survey dispersion and variance matching}
To preserve design-based sampling properties in a Bayesian GLMM, \pkg{fastsae} injects distribution-specific dispersion parameters:
%
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Gaussian}: Precision is fixed to the inverse design variance: $\tau_i = 1/D_i$.
  \item \textbf{Beta}: For proportions $y_i \in (0, 1)$ with logit link $\mathrm{logit}(\mu_i) = \eta_i$, the Beta variance is $\mathrm{Var}(y_i) = \mu_i(1 - \mu_i)/(1 + \phi_i)$. Equating this to design variance $D_i$ yields the \citet{janicki2020properties} dispersion:
  \begin{equation} \label{eq:beta_dispersion}
  \phi_i = \max\left( \frac{y_i (1 - y_i)}{D_i} - 1, \, 1 \right).
  \end{equation}
  \item \textbf{Gamma}: For positive skewed expenditures $y_i > 0$ with log link $\log(\mu_i) = \eta_i$, equating $\mathrm{Var}(y_i) = \mu_i^2 / s_i$ to $D_i$ yields the shape precision parameter:
  \begin{equation}
  s_i = \frac{y_i^2}{D_i} = \frac{1}{\mathrm{CV}_i^2}.
  \end{equation}
  \item \textbf{Binomial, Poisson, and Negative Binomial}: Domain sample sizes $n_i$ map to \code{Ntrials}, and population exposures $E_i$ map to the log-link offset $\log(E_i)$.
\end{enumerate}

\subsubsection{Spatial priors and space-time interactions}
The spatial random field $\bm{u}$ supports five graph topologies: Besag ICAR \citep{besag1991bayesian}, scaled BYM2 with Penalized Complexity (PC) priors \citep{riebler2016intuitive, simpson2017penalising}, Leroux CAR with exact precision $\bm{Q} = \tau[(1 - \rho)\bm{I} + \rho(\mathrm{diag}(\bm{d}) - \bm{W})]$ \citep{leroux2000estimation}, and the Spatial Lag Model (SLM). For panel observations across $T \ge 2$, temporal trends support random walks (RW1, RW2) and AR(1) dynamics. Space-time interactions can be specified as additive, domain-specific, separable, or according to the four \citet{knorrheld2000bayesian} interaction types ($\bm{Q}_{st} = \bm{Q}_s \otimes \bm{Q}_t$).

\subsection{Two-stage hierarchical benchmarking calibration} \label{sec:methods_bench}

In official statistics, model-based small area estimates $\hat{\theta}_{di}$ for domain $i$ in province/region $g$ must aggregate to officially published higher-level targets \citep{rao2015small, stefan2020benchmarking}. Let $w_{di}$ denote domain population weights such that $W_g = \sum_{i \in g} w_{di}$ is the provincial total weight, and $W_{\text{nat}} = \sum_g W_g$ is the national total weight. Let $T_g$ denote the provincial target and $T_{\text{nat}}$ denote the national aggregate target.

\pkg{fastsae} implements a two-stage hierarchical benchmarking calibration:
%
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Stage 1: Harmonization of Provincial Targets to National Benchmark}: When initial provincial targets $T_g$ (or initial aggregated model estimates $\hat{\bar{\theta}}_g = \sum_{i \in g} (w_{di}/W_g)\hat{\theta}_{di}$) do not sum to the national target $T_{\text{nat}}$, calibrated provincial targets $\tilde{T}_g$ are computed to satisfy $\sum_g (W_g / W_{\text{nat}}) \tilde{T}_g = T_{\text{nat}}$.
  \item \textbf{Stage 2: Domain-Level Calibration within Provinces}: Domain estimates within province $g$ are calibrated to satisfy $\sum_{i \in g} (w_{di}/W_g) \hat{\theta}_{di}^{\text{BM}} = \tilde{T}_g$.
\end{enumerate}
%
Across both stages, \pkg{fastsae} supports four calibration mechanisms:
%
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Ratio Benchmarking} \citep{you2002small}: $\hat{\theta}_{di}^{\text{BM}} = \hat{\theta}_{di} \left( \tilde{T}_g / \sum_{j \in g} \tilde{w}_{dj} \hat{\theta}_{dj} \right)$.
  \item \textbf{Difference Benchmarking} \citep{rao2015small}: $\hat{\theta}_{di}^{\text{BM}} = \hat{\theta}_{di} + \left( \tilde{T}_g - \sum_{j \in g} \tilde{w}_{dj} \hat{\theta}_{dj} \right)$.
  \item \textbf{Variance-Weighted Optimal Benchmarking} \citep{datta2011bayesian, steorts2014benchmarking}: Adjustments are allocated inversely proportional to domain precision, minimizing quadratic estimation loss:
  \begin{equation}
  \hat{\theta}_{di}^{\text{BM}} = \hat{\theta}_{di} + \mathrm{MSE}(\hat{\theta}_{di}) \left( \sum_{j \in g} \tilde{w}_{dj}^2 \mathrm{MSE}(\hat{\theta}_{dj}) \right)^{-1} \left( \tilde{T}_g - \sum_{j \in g} \tilde{w}_{dj} \hat{\theta}_{dj} \right).
  \end{equation}
  \item \textbf{Logit-Scale Benchmarking}: For bounded proportions in $(0, 1)$, linear adjustments can push estimates outside $[0, 1]$. \pkg{fastsae} solves for an additive shift $\alpha_g$ on the logit scale:
  \begin{equation}
  \sum_{i \in g} \tilde{w}_{di} \, \mathrm{logit}^{-1}\left( \mathrm{logit}(\hat{\theta}_{di}) + \alpha_g \right) = \tilde{T}_g,
  \end{equation}
  via robust one-dimensional root-finding, guaranteeing $\hat{\theta}_{di}^{\text{BM}} \in (0, 1)$.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Official statistics reliability classification} \label{sec:methods_reliability}

NSOs classify domain estimates into publication tiers based on the Relative Standard Error ($\mathrm{RSE} = \frac{\sqrt{\mathrm{MSE}(\hat{\theta}_i)}}{|\hat{\theta}_i|} \times 100\%$). \pkg{fastsae} provides \code{add\_reliability\_flags()} implementing institutional thresholding:
%
\begin{equation} \label{eq:reliability}
\mathrm{Tier}_i = \begin{cases}
\text{Reliable (Direct Publish)}, & \mathrm{RSE}_i < c_1, \\
\text{Use with Caution (Accompanying Warning)}, & c_1 \le \mathrm{RSE}_i < c_2, \\
\text{Unreliable (Suppress or Aggregate)}, & \mathrm{RSE}_i \ge c_2,
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
%
where default thresholds $(c_1, c_2) = (20\%, 30\%)$ or $(25\%, 50\%)$ conform to standards adopted by agencies such as BPS-Statistics Indonesia and Eurostat.

%% -- Software Design ----------------------------------------------------------

\section{Software design and implementation} \label{sec:design}

The architecture of \pkg{fastsae} unites compiled linear algebra with high-level Bayesian GMRF modeling and production reporting. Figure~\ref{fig:arch} outlines the structural flow.

\begin{figure}[t!]
\centering
\begin{framed}
\centering
\textbf{\pkg{fastsae} High-Level Software Architecture}\\[6pt]
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\begin{tabular}{p{4.2cm}p{4.8cm}p{4.2cm}}
\toprule
\multicolumn{3}{c}{\textbf{1. User-Facing Production API (\proglang{R})}} \\
\midrule
\textbf{Frequentist Modeling} & \textbf{Bayesian HB Engine} & \textbf{Production Workflow} \\
\code{eblup\_fh()} & \code{hb\_area(..., family = "beta")} & \code{benchmark\_sae()} (Two-Stage) \\
\code{eblup\_sfh()} & \code{hb\_area(..., family = "gamma")} & \code{compare\_sae()} \\
\code{eblup\_stfh()} & \code{hb\_area(..., family = "poisson")} & \code{add\_reliability\_flags()} \\
\code{eblup\_bhf()} & \code{hb\_area(..., spatial = "bym2")} & \code{map\_sae()} / \code{export\_sae()} \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{c}{\textbf{2. Core Computational Backends}} \\
\midrule
\textbf{Compiled C++17 Core} & \textbf{INLA Deterministic Engine} & \textbf{Spatial Linear Algebra} \\
\pkg{RcppArmadillo} Matrix Algebra & Sparse Cholesky Factorization & Row-normalized Proximity \\
OpenMP Threaded Bootstrap & Full Laplace Approximation & PC Priors \& GMRF Graphs \\
In-Place Fisher-Scoring Updates & Survey Dispersion Injections & Spectral Eigenvalue Bounds \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{framed}
\caption{\label{fig:arch} Structural architecture and functional components of \pkg{fastsae}.}
\end{figure}

The frequentist engines (\code{eblup\_fh()}, \code{eblup\_sfh()}, \code{eblup\_stfh()}, \code{eblup\_bhf()}) avoid temporary matrix allocations inside REML loops by reusing working buffers in-place. The bootstrap MSE loops are decorated with OpenMP pragmas (\code{\#pragma omp parallel for}), delivering multi-core throughput scaling. The Bayesian engine (\code{hb\_area()}) automates the translation of formulas, spatial adjacency matrices $\bm{W}$, and survey dispersion arguments into \pkg{R-INLA} calls. Standard S3 methods (\code{print()}, \code{summary()}, \code{plot()}, \code{coef()}, \code{fitted()}, \code{residuals()}) are implemented across all model classes.

%% -- Computational Benchmarks -------------------------------------------------

\section{Computational benchmarks and simulations} \label{sec:simulations}

\subsection{Frequentist scalability and memory efficiency: fastsae vs. sae and emdi}

We evaluated computational speed and peak memory consumption across domain sizes $D \in \{30, 50, 100, 250, 500, 1000\}$ using simulated datasets with 5 auxiliary covariates. Benchmarks were conducted using the \pkg{bench} package on an Apple Silicon processor under \proglang{R}~4.4.2.

\begin{table}[t!]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{rcccccc}
\toprule
$D$ & \multicolumn{3}{c}{Execution Time: Median (seconds)} & \multicolumn{3}{c}{Peak Memory Footprint (MB)} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-7}
 & \pkg{fastsae} & \pkg{sae} & \pkg{emdi} & \pkg{fastsae} & \pkg{sae} & \pkg{emdi} \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{7}{l}{\textit{Fay-Herriot EBLUP (Classical)}} \\
30   & 0.00059 & 0.00155 & 0.01088 & 0.005 & 0.153 & 4.18 \\
100  & 0.00055 & 0.00344 & 0.05051 & 0.016 & 0.918 & 37.34 \\
500  & 0.00140 & 0.19673 & 5.86366 & 0.076 & 18.239 & 880.31 \\
1000 & 0.00413 & 1.50363 & 51.0224 & 0.186 & 71.977 & 3,773.60 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{7}{l}{\textit{Spatial Fay-Herriot (SEBLUP)}} \\
30   & 0.00073 & 0.00410 & 0.01071 & 0.030 & 1.373 & 4.18 \\
100  & 0.00473 & 0.07054 & 0.05299 & 0.259 & 17.976 & 37.34 \\
500  & 0.14205 & 7.11793 & 3.13662 & 5.857 & 776.407 & 949.86 \\
1000 & 0.83216 & 67.1518 & 51.0593 & 23.155 & 1,920.01 & 3,772.57 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{7}{l}{\textit{Spatio-Temporal Fay-Herriot (STEBLUP, $T = 5$)}} \\
30   & 0.03674 & 0.11856 & -- & 0.027 & 43.801 & -- \\
100  & 2.29928 & 17.9425 & -- & 0.090 & 2,551.10 & -- \\
500  & 10.3596 & 253.343 & -- & 0.448 & 8,124.37 & -- \\
1000 & 58.1005 & 1,786.49 & -- & 0.894 & 32,478.3 & -- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{\label{tab:bench_freq} Frequentist runtime and peak memory benchmark across domain sample sizes ($D$). \pkg{fastsae} avoids quadratic RAM growth and achieves up to $364\times$ speedup for FH, $80\times$ for SFH, and $31\times$ for ST-FH at $D=1,000$.}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:bench_freq} demonstrates the dramatic scalability gains of \pkg{fastsae}. At $D=1,000$, classical FH completes in 4 milliseconds in \pkg{fastsae} compared to 1.5 seconds in \pkg{sae} ($364\times$ slower) and 51 seconds in \pkg{emdi} ($12,343\times$ slower). For spatio-temporal models at $D=1,000$, \pkg{sae} required nearly 30 minutes (1,786 seconds) and consumed 32.5 gigabytes of RAM, whereas \pkg{fastsae} finished in 58 seconds using under 1 megabyte of memory.
\textbf{Operational Takeaway}: \pkg{fastsae} eliminates out-of-memory bottlenecks, enabling routine spatio-temporal estimation across thousands of domains on standard laptop hardware.

\subsection{Bayesian Beta Hierarchical Modeling scalability: fastsae vs. tipsae}

For bounded rates, we benchmarked \code{hb\_area(..., family = "beta")} against \pkg{tipsae} \citep{denicolo2022tipsae} using Stan (4 chains, 2,000 iterations).

\begin{table}[t!]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{rcccccc}
\toprule
$D$ & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Cross-Sectional Beta} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Spatial Beta (Besag)} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Spatio-Temporal Beta} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \cmidrule(lr){4-5} \cmidrule(lr){6-7}
 & \pkg{fastsae} & \pkg{tipsae} & \pkg{fastsae} & \pkg{tipsae} & \pkg{fastsae} & \pkg{tipsae} \\
\midrule
30   & 1.24 s & 2.35 s   & 0.61 s & 25.72 s  & 1.44 s & 540.57 s \\
50   & 1.33 s & 3.77 s   & 0.62 s & 65.72 s  & 1.67 s & 908.17 s \\
100  & 1.27 s & 8.24 s   & 1.37 s & 100.02 s & --     & --       \\
250  & 1.31 s & 26.28 s  & 1.32 s & 155.90 s & --     & --       \\
500  & 1.41 s & 64.21 s  & 1.43 s & 248.82 s & --     & --       \\
1000 & 1.64 s & 187.16 s & 1.73 s & 404.34 s & --     & --       \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{\label{tab:bench_beta} Execution time comparison for Bayesian Beta models. Point estimates between \pkg{fastsae} and \pkg{tipsae} achieve $r = 0.9993$ concordance.}
\end{table}

As shown in Table~\ref{tab:bench_beta}, deterministic Laplace approximations in \pkg{fastsae} scale flatly with domain count: fitting a spatial Beta model at $D=1,000$ requires only 1.73 seconds in \pkg{fastsae} compared to 404 seconds in \pkg{tipsae} ($234\times$ speedup). In spatio-temporal panel models ($D=50, T=5$), \pkg{tipsae} required 15.1 minutes (908 seconds), whereas \pkg{fastsae} completed in 1.67 seconds ($544\times$ speedup). Despite this speed differential, inferential concordance on $D=1,000$ yields Pearson correlation $r = 0.9993$, Spearman $\rho = 0.9992$, and Mean Absolute Difference $\mathrm{MAD} = 0.0197$.
\textbf{Operational Takeaway}: Deterministic Laplace approximation in \pkg{fastsae} achieves inferential equivalence with fully converged Hamiltonian MCMC in seconds rather than minutes or hours.

\subsection{Monte Carlo finite population validation against ground truth}

To assess frequentist design properties, we conducted design-based Monte Carlo simulations under known ground truth at national scale ($D = 500$ small areas, $R = 25$ independent sample draws) using a synthetic finite population of $N = 250,000$ individuals.

\begin{table}[t!]
\centering
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{5pt}
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Estimator} & \textbf{Emp. RMSE} & \textbf{RRMSE (\%)} & \textbf{MARB (\%)} & \textbf{95\% CrI Cov.} & \textbf{CrI Width} & \textbf{Runtime} \\
\midrule
Direct Sample Estimator  & 0.0719 & 27.1\% & 21.7\% & -- & -- & -- \\
\pkg{fastsae} (Spatial Besag) & \textbf{0.0481} & \textbf{19.1\%} & 16.9\% & 89.0\% & \textbf{0.173} & \textbf{4.17 s} \\
\pkg{tipsae} (Spatial Besag)  & 0.0487 & 18.7\% & \textbf{15.7\%} & \textbf{93.7\%} & 0.202 & 36.86 s \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{\label{tab:mc_truth} Finite population Monte Carlo simulation results ($D = 500$, $R = 25$). \pkg{fastsae} achieves an efficiency gain of $1.92\times$ over the direct survey estimator.}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:mc_truth} demonstrates that \code{hb\_area()} reduces empirical RMSE from 0.0719 (direct survey estimator) to 0.0481, yielding a $1.92\times$ empirical efficiency gain. Relative Root Mean Squared Error drops from $27.1\%$ to $19.1\%$. Comparing Bayesian engines, \pkg{fastsae} achieves a slightly lower empirical RMSE than \pkg{tipsae} (0.0481 vs. 0.0487) and produces narrower credible intervals (0.173 vs. 0.202) while requiring nearly $9\times$ less runtime per fit.
\textbf{Operational Takeaway}: \pkg{fastsae} produces well-calibrated, highly efficient area-level predictions that closely track true finite-population parameters while scaling effortlessly to national-scale administrative systems.

%% -- Applied Illustration -----------------------------------------------------

\section{Applied illustration and production workflow} \label{sec:applications}

We demonstrate an end-to-end NSO production workflow using the benchmark dataset \code{sim\_area} ($D = 42$ domains across 6 provincial groups).

\subsection{Loading data, spatial weights, and fitting models}
<<load_and_fit>>=
library("fastsae")
data("sim_area")
sim_area$prov <- rep(paste0("Prov_", 1:6), each = 7)
sim_area$pop <- round(runif(nrow(sim_area), 15000, 45000))

# Construct row-standardized spatial proximity matrix
coords <- cbind(sim_area$x_coord, sim_area$y_coord)
dists <- as.matrix(dist(coords))
W <- (dists < quantile(dists, 0.18)) * 1
diag(W) <- 0
rs <- rowSums(W); rs[rs == 0] <- 1; W_std <- W / rs

# Fit Frequentist and Bayesian Models
fit_fh <- eblup_fh(y_gaussian ~ x1 + x2, vardir = "vardir",
  data = sim_area, method = "REML", print_result = FALSE)
fit_sfh <- eblup_sfh(y_gaussian ~ x1 + x2, vardir = "vardir",
  domain = "area", W = W_std, data = sim_area,
  method = "REML", print_result = FALSE)
fit_beta <- hb_area(y_beta ~ x1 + x2, vardir = "vardir",
  data = sim_area, family = "beta", spatial = "bym2",
  W = W, print_result = FALSE)
@

\subsection{Automated model concordance evaluation}
Practitioners can immediately compare competitive models via \code{compare\_sae()}:
<<compare_models>>=
comp <- compare_sae(fit_fh, fit_sfh, names = c("FH", "SFH"))
comp$metrics[c(1, 2, 4, 6, 8, 9), ]
@

\subsection{Two-stage hierarchical benchmarking calibration}
To harmonize domain estimates with an official national poverty target ($T_{\text{nat}} = 0.50$), we apply two-stage calibration:
<<hierarchical_benchmark>>=
bm <- benchmark_sae(fit_fh, national_target = 0.50, group = "prov",
  weight = "pop", method = "ratio", type = "mean")
head(bm[, c("domain", "group", "original", "benchmarked", "rel_adjustment_pct")], 4)
@
The calibrated estimates satisfy $\sum (w_i / \sum w_i) \hat{\theta}_i^{\text{BM}} = 0.50$ exactly.

\subsection{Official statistics reliability classification}
Using \code{add\_reliability\_flags()}, domains are classified into official dissemination tiers:
<<reliability_flags>>=
flags <- add_reliability_flags(fit_beta, thresholds = c(20, 30))
table(flags$reliability)
@

\subsection{Model diagnostics and visualization}
Diagnostics via \code{diagnose()} and \code{autoplot()} provide visual quality verification:
\begin{figure}[t!]
\centering
<<plot_diagnostics, echo=TRUE, fig=TRUE, height=4.5, width=7>>=
diag_beta <- diagnose(fit_beta)
print(autoplot(diag_beta))
@
\caption{\label{fig:diag_plot} Model diagnostic panels generated via \code{autoplot.fastsae\_diagnose()}.}
\end{figure}

%% -- Summary and Discussion ---------------------------------------------------

\section{Summary and discussion} \label{sec:summary}

Small area estimation in official statistics requires reconciling mathematical rigor with strict computational and operational constraints. \pkg{fastsae} resolves these challenges by bridging high-performance compiled linear algebra, deterministic Bayesian approximations, and production-ready dissemination tools.

\begin{table}[t!]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{p{3.0cm}p{4.5cm}p{6.8cm}}
\toprule
\textbf{Dimension} & \textbf{Observed Empirical Evidence} & \textbf{Institutional Implication (``So What'')} \\
\midrule
\textbf{Frequentist Scalability} & $364\times$ speedup for FH and $80\times$ for SFH; RAM under 25 MB at $D=1,000$ (vs 32 GB in \pkg{sae}). & Eliminates server memory exhaustion, allowing desktop computers to execute nationwide bootstrap MSE routines. \\
\addlinespace
\textbf{Bayesian EBP via INLA} & $73\times$ to $544\times$ speedup over Stan MCMC; point correlation $r = 0.9993$ on $D=1,000$. & Eliminates manual MCMC chain diagnostics and divergent transition failures in routine production timelines. \\
\addlinespace
\textbf{Design-Based Ground Truth} & $1.92\times$ empirical efficiency gain over direct estimator; $89\%$ empirical coverage. & Delivers substantial MSE reductions while maintaining nominal interval coverage under repeated sampling. \\
\addlinespace
\textbf{Hierarchical Benchmarking} & Simultaneous Level 0 and Level 1 target alignment with logit boundary safety. & Guarantees numeric consistency with published aggregates, eliminating public confusion without violating probability bounds. \\
\addlinespace
\textbf{Turnkey Dissemination} & Direct RSE classification, spatial choropleths, and multi-sheet exports. & Collapses multi-script analyst workflows into standardized, reproducible publication pipelines. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{\label{tab:synthesis} Synthesis of empirical findings and operational implications of \pkg{fastsae}.}
\end{table}

As synthesized in Table~\ref{tab:synthesis}, \pkg{fastsae} provides substantial efficiency gains across both frequentist and Bayesian paradigms. Future developments will focus on expanding unit-level spatial EBP under non-Gaussian likelihoods, integrating zero-inflated Beta (ZIB) specifications for extreme sparse indicators, and supporting direct spatial polygon streaming.

%% -- Computational Details & Acknowledgments ----------------------------------

\section*{Computational details}

The results in this paper were obtained using \proglang{R}~4.4.2 with \pkg{fastsae}~0.1.0, \pkg{R-INLA}~24.06.27, \pkg{sae}~1.3, \pkg{emdi}~2.2.1, \pkg{tipsae}~1.0.1, and \pkg{ggplot2}~3.5.1. \proglang{R} and packages from CRAN are available at \url{https://CRAN.R-project.org/}. \pkg{R-INLA} is available from \url{https://www.r-inla.org/}. The \pkg{fastsae} package is available on GitHub at \url{https://github.com/ridsonap/fastsae}.

